# Part 1 — Audit of the current 3D presentation (Goal step 1)

## 1.1 Ownership and render chain

```
routes/Root.tsx:129            mounts <SystemCoreDialog /> once, app-wide
  store/misc.ts:79             Recoil atom `showSystemCore` opens it
  Chat/SystemCoreButton.tsx    raises the atom (currently ungated)
  hooks/Nav/useUnifiedSidebarLinks.ts:77   also raises it

SystemCore/Dialog.tsx          owns open/scannerVisible/panelOpen/resetToken
  hooks/SystemCore/useModules.ts   owns modules[] + the runtime Map
  Boundary + Suspense + lazy(Scene)   render-gated on `open`
  Panel.tsx                    fully controlled: props in, callbacks out
```

Two properties worth preserving explicitly:

- **The `three` chunk is not fetched until the modal opens.** `Scene.tsx` is the
  lazy boundary's payload; `resolve.ts:8-13` documents that it must stay free of
  `three` imports precisely so the dialog shell does not drag the library into
  the eager bundle.
- **`Panel.tsx` is fully controlled.** All mutation goes through callbacks from
  `useModules`. This is what makes the editor changes in Part 5 small.

## 1.2 The data→visual seam exists and is good

`scene/resolve.ts` (86 lines) is the single place a `Module` becomes numbers:

| Function | Produces |
|---|---|
| `colorOf(m)` | `appearance.color` override, else `STATUS[m.status].color` |
| `gainOf(m)` | `STATUS[m.status].gain ?? 1` |
| `stripSpec(m, rt)` | flat `StripSpec` — geometry, motion, appearance, audio, lane |
| `newRuntime(m, laneSeq)` | starting phase and lane |

It has exactly **two** consumers, which is why extending it is safe:

- `Scene.tsx:28` imports them; `:175` calls `stripSpec`; `:179` calls
  `mats.forModule(colorOf(m), m.appearance.opacity, gainOf(m))`
- `useModules.ts:21,58` calls `newRuntime`

## 1.3 Periodic updates are already structurally safe

This is the most important audit finding, because it means no rearchitecting is
required to support a poll.

`Strip.tsx:11-22` documents three rules and all three hold:

1. `rotation.y` is never passed as a prop, so no re-render can reset the live
   angle. It is set once in a callback ref and thereafter only by `useFrame`.
2. `Scene.tsx:213` keys strips by `strip.spec.id`, so new props re-render a
   strip without remounting it and without touching its siblings.
3. The live angle is parked every frame into a `Map` held in a ref
   (`useModules.ts:66-71`), so even a genuine remount resumes rather than
   snapping back.

`Scene.tsx:171` memoizes `strips` on `[modules, runtimeFor, mats, fontReady]`.
**Replacing the `modules` array therefore re-renders without remounting and
without dropping rotation.** A poll is already supported by construction.

## 1.4 What is *not* data-driven — the actual gap

1. **No live source.** `data/fixture.ts` loads 26 modules from
   `data/modules.json`. Three comments say the real source is out of scope:
   `fixture.ts:4`, `useModules.ts:15`, `Dialog.tsx:19`.

2. **`telemetry` is a declared-but-dead field.** `data/schema.ts:121` declares
   `telemetry: { progress: number|null; health: number|null; metrics: Record<string, number> }`,
   with full form controls (`schema.ts:352-376`) and serialization
   (`serialize.ts:42-46`). Nothing in `scene/` or `objects/` reads it, and every
   module in `modules.json` omits it.

3. **Only `Strip` is data-driven.** `Mainframe`, `Scanner`, `Orbit` and `Hum`
   take no data at all — only `animate`, `bus`, `controlsRef`. `Contact` and
   `Tone` are driven, but via `Strip`.

4. **`Contact` is not rendered.** `Strip.tsx:253-258` has the `<Contact>` block
   commented out. So `StripSpec.lane` currently drives nothing visible, the
   scanner deck shows its grid but no contacts, and any lane or overflow-lane
   work is invisible until that block is restored.

## 1.5 Three unbounded caches that live data would leak through

These are the concrete reasons a naive "bind a metric to a colour" would fail.

### `MaterialRegistry` — up to 130 GPU materials per tick

`scene/materials.ts:96` holds `private readonly reg = new Map<string, THREE.MeshStandardMaterial>()`,
keyed at `:105` on `kind|colorHex|opacity|gain`. There is no eviction and no
`dispose()` outside the whole-registry teardown at `:164`.

`forModule()` (`:124-132`) requests **five** materials — band, glow, halo, hot,
mark. `Scene.tsx:179` calls it once per module inside the `strips` memo. At 26
modules that is **130 `get()` calls per data change**.

With the authored fixture every call hits the cache: ~5 statuses produce ~25
distinct materials, allocated once. If a *continuous* signal drove colour or
opacity, each tick could miss on all 130 and allocate 130 new
`MeshStandardMaterial` — GPU resources never released. At a 20 s poll that is
~390/minute, ~23k/hour for a dialog left open.

### `LabelFactory` — worse, because it holds textures

`scene/labels.ts:73` holds `private readonly cache = new Map<string, THREE.MeshBasicMaterial>()`,
one entry per distinct string, each backed by a `CanvasTexture`, plus a
synchronous `measureText` loop on every miss. **Never bind band text to a
number.** Same rule as materials, with a larger constant.

### `Tone` — audible pumping, not just cost

`objects/Tone.tsx:38`: `useMemo(() => bus.roar(hz, level), [bus, hz, level])`,
with `bus.release(audio)` on cleanup at `:42`.

`ToneBus.roar()` (`scene/audio.ts:133`) builds a full graph per voice: a lowpass
biquad, a `WaveShaper` with a computed curve at `4x` oversample, a churn gain, a
highpass biquad, a looping white-noise `BufferSource`, plus modulators.
`release()` (`:319`) cancels scheduled values, ramps gain to zero over
`ROAR.fadeOut` (0.25 s), and only then stops the sources — and `audio.ts:102`
notes that overlapping voices sum to **double amplitude**.

Binding `audio.hz`/`audio.level` would tear down and rebuild ~10 nodes per
module per tick with fades overlapping the new voices: 26 rebuilds every tick,
pumping the scene's level audibly.

## 1.6 Pre-existing breakage, and its cause

`npx jest src/components/SystemCore` in `client/` → **3 suites failed, 6 tests
failed, 76 passed.**

```
data/__tests__/schema.spec.ts   expects 25 modules, fixture has 26   (x2)
__tests__/Dialog.spec.tsx:59    expects text "(25)"
__tests__/Panel.spec.tsx:38     expects text "(25)"
__tests__/Panel.spec.tsx:50     expects Radius 0.62, fixture has 0.72
__tests__/Panel.spec.tsx:101    expects "(26)" after an add
```

The untracked `modules.json` grew to 26 modules and `kernel-scheduler`'s radius
changed to `0.72`; the tracked specs were never updated, and nothing caught it.

**Root cause:** `.gitignore:6` is a bare `data/`, which ignores the whole of
`client/src/components/SystemCore/data/`. Six real source files — `schema.ts`
(701 lines), `status.ts`, `fixture.ts`, `serialize.ts`, `modules.json`, and
`data/__tests__/schema.spec.ts` — are untracked. 25 of 31 SystemCore files are
tracked; these six are not.

The rule sits under `### node etc ###` beside `data-node` and `meili_data*`, and
**no `data/` directory exists at the repo root**, so it was plainly meant to be
root-anchored. Recommended fix: change it to `/data/` and
`git add client/src/components/SystemCore/data/`. Blast radius is exactly those
six files plus `.claude-flow/data/` (already ignored separately at `.gitignore:197`)
and `coverage/` build artifacts.

**Consequences for this design:** no new code goes under any `data/` directory,
and `data/status.ts` cannot gain a new status value until this is fixed.

