# Part 4 — Periodic updates (Goal step 3)

## 4.1 Backend snapshot pipeline — `packages/api/src/systemCore/`

`config.ts`, `queries.ts`, `catalog.ts`, `scale.ts`, `assemble.ts`,
`snapshot.ts`, `handler.ts`, `index.ts`, each with a spec.

Depth is deliberate:

| Module | Interface | Hides |
|---|---|---|
| `snapshot.ts` | `getSystemCoreSnapshot(): Promise<Response>` — **zero parameters** | config, 14 HTTP calls, concurrency, timeouts, TTL cache, coalescing, serve-stale, partial failure, catalogue matching, normalization |
| `config.ts` | `getSystemCoreConfig(env?)` | env names, URL validation, clamping |
| `assemble.ts` | pure `assembleSnapshot(results, config, now)` | matching, expansion, overflow, truncation |
| `scale.ts` | pure `normalize`, `deriveStatus`, `deriveHealth` | clamping, NaN/Inf handling, polarity |
| `handler.ts` | thin | HTTP status and headers only |

**The zero-parameter signature is the load-bearing security decision.** There is
no parameter through which a request field could reach PromQL, so "never accept
client-supplied PromQL" is a property of the type system, not a validation rule
someone can forget to call. `handler.ts` never imports `queries.ts`.

Reinforced three ways: the route takes no query string, path params or body;
every `expr` is a string literal, asserted by a spec that fails the build on any
backtick or `${`; and dropping the `namespace=` filters from the cAdvisor queries
(they cost nothing at 37 series and serve the exporter-less namespaces for free)
removes the last expression that would have wanted a config value interpolated
into PromQL.

**Execution.** POST `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` to `/api/v1/query` — the
bundle expressions run to ~600 chars, so POST avoids URL-length and proxy-log
concerns. Per-request `AbortSignal.timeout()` *and* Prometheus's own `timeout`
param slightly below it, so the server abandons an evaluation we have stopped
listening to. Concurrency 6 → three waves → ~50 ms wall clock.

**Partial failure.** `Promise.allSettled` over all 14. A failed query marks
exactly the channels bound to it as `error` plus one `errors[]` entry; everything
else resolves normally. Note the asymmetry that makes this work: an *exporter*
being down is not a failure at all — the query succeeds with an empty result, so
the channel is `missing`. Failures come only from Prometheus itself.

**Cache and coalescing.** Module-level, in-process: a TTL cache plus a
single-flight promise. Ten admins polling in the same window produce one set of
14 upstream queries. Default TTL 25 s — below the 30 s scrape interval so a poll
cannot systematically skip a scrape, and high enough that upstream load is
**0.56 q/s regardless of admin count**. In-process rather than the shared Keyv
cache because this is ephemeral derived data whose payload would need
serializing on every hit, and per-replica caching only multiplies upstream load
by the replica count (still negligible at 2–3 replicas).

**Serve-stale.** If a refresh fails entirely and the last good snapshot is
younger than `staleMaxMs`, return it with its true `cacheAgeMs` and every module
flagged stale. A 3D scene going dark for one dropped packet is worse than one
showing 40-second-old data labelled as such.

**Always HTTP 200 when configured.** This is a deliberate divergence from
`admin/langfuse.ts:97-103`, which 404s when unavailable, and the file header
should say so. Rationale: React Query treats non-2xx as an error and would
discard the good half of a partial snapshot, forcing the client to reconstruct
partial-failure semantics out of an error object. A 200 whose body says "off" or
"half-broken" keeps the whole state machine in one typed payload.

| Situation | Response |
|---|---|
| Env unset | 200, `configured:false, reason:'unset'`, no upstream traffic |
| Env malformed | 200, `configured:false, reason:'invalid_url'` |
| Unreachable, no prior snapshot | 200, all modules present with authored placement, all channels `error`, status `loading` |
| Unreachable, recent snapshot | 200, last good snapshot, `stale:true`, errors appended |
| One bundle 400s | 200, only that bundle's channels `error` |

**Config.** One switch, `SYSTEM_CORE_PROMETHEUS_URL` — unset means off, so the
enable and the destination cannot disagree. Optional
`SYSTEM_CORE_{TIMEOUT_MS,CACHE_TTL_MS,SCRAPE_INTERVAL_SECONDS,STALE_MAX_MS}`,
all clamped. URL validation follows `packages/api/src/rum/proxy.ts:33-56`: parse
via `new URL`, reject credentials, query and fragment, require http/https.

**On not using `isSSRFTarget`.** That validator
(`packages/api/src/auth/domain.ts:95-170`) exists to stop *user-supplied* URLs
from reaching internal infrastructure. Here the URL is operator-supplied server
config at the same trust level as `MONGO_URI`, and reaching internal
infrastructure is the entire point. Routing through it would break the feature
three ways over: the in-cluster hostname is literally `prometheus`, denylisted at
`domain.ts:148`; `.svc.cluster.local` is caught by the `.local` TLD rule; and the
resolved address is cluster-private. `rum/proxy.ts` already does exactly this for
the OTLP collector — verified, it does not import `isSSRFTarget` — so this is an
established pattern, not a new exemption.

No Helm change needed: `helm/librechat/templates/configmap-env.yaml` already
renders `configEnv` verbatim.

## 4.2 The client poll — `client/src/data-provider/SystemCore/queries.ts`

Modelled on `useActiveJobs` (`SSE/queries.ts:227-244`) with the module-level
error-budget breaker from `Files/queries.ts:187-231`.

```ts
const POLL_MS = 20_000;        // one tick short of the scrape interval
const POLL_FLOOR_MS = 10_000;  // a hostile nextPollMs must not hammer
export const SNAPSHOT_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_ERRORS = 4;
```

Options and their justifications:

- **20 s, not 30 s.** Polling *at* the scrape interval beats against scrape
  phase and drops one sample per drift cycle. 20 s guarantees at most one poll
  per sample. Duplicate ticks are nearly free — after quantization every derived
  channel is identical, so `forModule` returns the same object identities and
  R3F writes nothing to the GPU.
- **`staleTime` = interval**, which is what makes the three `refetchOn*: true`
  settings safe: a reopen or a window focus inside one interval reuses the cache
  instead of firing an extra request.
- **`retry: false`** — a retried poll is a duplicate sample, the next tick *is*
  the retry, and retries would corrupt the consecutive-error count.
- **`refetchOnWindowFocus`/`OnReconnect: true`** — background tabs get their
  timers throttled, so returning to a stale tab is exactly when a refetch earns
  its keep.
- **`enabled: (config?.enabled ?? true) === true && queriesEnabled`** — required,
  or the poll fires on the login screen and trips `request.ts`'s 401 →
  `redirectToLoginOnce()`.
- **Server-driven `nextPollMs`** with a client floor, via the function form of
  `refetchInterval` that both existing precedents use.
- **The breaker is counted manually** because React Query v4 resets
  `fetchFailureCount` on every fetch dispatch and so cannot count across polls —
  the same reason `Files/queries.ts:187-190` keeps its own counter. It latches
  after 4 consecutive failures (~80 s) and is reopened by a Panel retry button
  and by the false→true transition of `open`, since reopening the modal is the
  natural retry gesture. Unlike a file preview this poll has no terminal state,
  so a latch with no way out would be a dead scene.

**Polling only while the dialog is open.** `useLive({ enabled: available && open })`
is called from `Dialog.tsx`, *above* the `open &&` render gate — which is fine,
because the query's `enabled` gates the network, not the mount. The hook must
**not** move into `Scene`, which is the lazy `three`-only chunk; data has no
business there. The default 5-minute `cacheTime` means reopening within five
minutes paints the last snapshot instantly and refetches in the background,
matching the intent already stated at `Dialog.tsx:17-19`.

## 4.3 How a tick reaches the GPU without a remount

1. `query.data` gets a new identity → the `readings` memo recomputes (26 `Map`
   inserts).
2. The `catalog` memo does **not** recompute — keyed on `catalogRevision` — so
   `reconcile` does not fire and `modules` keeps its identity.
3. `strips` recomputes: per module one runtime lookup, ~20 flops in `bind`, one
   `StripSpec` literal, five `MaterialRegistry` lookups.
4. `Strip` re-renders but does **not remount**, because `key={strip.spec.id}` is
   unchanged.
5. `rotation.y` is not a prop, so React writes nothing to it. The `attach`
   callback has `[]` deps. `initialPhase` changing is harmless — it is read only
   at mount.
6. `rate` is recomputed from the new `spec.speed`; `useFrame` re-registers its
   callback each render anyway, so the new rate is live next frame with no
   re-subscribe.
7. The label memo's deps are all authored and unchanged → no rasterisation.
8. Material props are the *same object identities* whenever the status/opacity/
   gain bucket is unchanged, so R3F's `applyProps` writes nothing.
9. Post-commit, `mats.sweep()` runs once.

**Cost at 26 modules:** ~130 `Map` lookups, ~52 small object literals, a few
hundred prop comparisons, and in the steady state **zero GPU uploads** — well
under a millisecond, once per 20 s, against a 60 fps loop.

**The one unverified assumption:** step 3 creates fresh `args` arrays for
`cylinderGeometry`, and this design assumes R3F compares them element-wise and
does not rebuild the geometry. It is already true today (editing one module
re-renders its strip without visible churn), but it is measured rather than
argued — see Part 7.

## 4.4 The bound set in v1, and why it is tiny

| Path | Channel | Codomain | Allocations/tick |
|---|---|---|---|
| `status` | `up` / `health` | **5 keys from `STATUS`** | 0 |
| `motion.speed` | `rate` | a number, rounded to 3 dp | 0 |
| `telemetry.health` | `health` | readout only | 0 |
| `telemetry.progress` | `load` | readout only | 0 |

Everything else — geometry, lane, labels, audio, colour override, opacity,
glow/halo/trail, visibility — stays authored and fully editable.

**This is the whole answer to the material leak.** Colour and gain flow
`status → STATUS[key] → colorOf/gainOf`. If the only live input to colour is a
*status key*, the colour channel's codomain is the five-entry authored palette,
and the registry can hold no more than it holds today. The leak is closed **by
construction, not by cleanup.** State it as an invariant in `bind.ts`:

> Every channel that reaches `MaterialRegistry.get()` must have a finite,
> enumerable codomain declared as a constant in this file.

`motion.speed` allocates nothing: it lands at `Strip.tsx:102` and is read by the
already-running `useFrame` closure. No material, no geometry, no audio node.

### Curves and clamping

```
health  → clamp01, null-safe, NaN → null
speed   → sign(authored) * clamp(BASE + K * log1p(rate / SCALE), MIN, MAX)
```

Two things matter, and both are testable:

1. **The clamp bounds are the schema's bounds.** `motion.speed` is
   `min: -10, max: 10` (`schema.ts:198-201`). Bound values never enter a
   `Module`, so nothing validates them — an out-of-range speed would make the
   stack a blur. Tests must read the limits from `SPEC` rather than hard-coding
   them, so `bind.ts` and the schema cannot drift.
2. **The authored value is the anchor, not an alternative.** `boundSpeed`
   preserves `sign(m.motion.speed)`, so a module authored to spin the other way
   keeps doing so and the signal changes only *how fast*. Every future channel
   takes the same shape: **live modulates authored, it does not replace it.**
   That is what keeps a hand edit meaningful on a bound field.

### Status derivation

```
m.status === 'controller' → 'controller'      // identity, not health
!up                       → 'fault'
health == null            → m.status          // no signal is not bad news
health < 0.34             → 'fault'
health < 0.67             → 'loading'         // stands in for 'degraded'
otherwise                 → 'running'
```

No hysteresis in v1, deliberately: the material key space is bounded at five
statuses, so a value flapping across a threshold costs **zero allocations** — a
cosmetic annoyance, not a leak. Smoothing belongs in the backend's PromQL window
(`avg_over_time`), where it is one expression instead of client state.

### Belt-and-braces: a generation sweep on `MaterialRegistry`

A bounded codomain is a convention, and conventions rot. Add ~25 lines: stamp
`userData.gen` on hit *and* miss; `sweep()` disposes anything untouched for two
generations, then opens a new generation; expose a `size` getter for tests and a
`logger.warn` past a cap.

Three details matter:

- **Two generations of grace, not one** — a value that disappears for one tick
  and returns must not be rebuilt.
- **`dispose()` alone is unsafe**: a disposed material still referenced by a live
  mesh renders black. Call `sweep()` from a **post-commit effect**
  (`useEffect(() => mats.sweep(), [strips, mats])`), never during render and
  never per frame, so nothing disposed was used in the render that just landed.
- `setDimmed` and `pulse` iterate `reg.values()` per frame and simply see fewer
  entries after a sweep.

### Audio: not bound in v1

`audio.hz`/`audio.level` stay authored, so they change only on a hand edit —
exactly when rebuilding a voice is the correct behaviour. Zero churn, and
`Tone.tsx` needs no change.

If audio is ever bound, in order of preference:

1. **`ToneBus.setLevel(audio, level)`** writing into the existing voice's
   `AudioParam`. `roar()` bakes the level in at `audio.ts:213` and `Voice` is
   private, so today a level change *must* rebuild. The bus already glides via
   `setTargetAtTime` with `ROAR.glide = { level: 0.09, cutoff: 0.14 }`
   (`tone.ts`), so this is inaudible as a transition and costs one param write
   instead of ~10 node constructions. `Tone` then keys its memo on `[bus, hz]`.
2. **Quantize `hz` with hysteresis.** Hysteresis is the important half:
   quantization alone does not stop a value oscillating across a bucket edge
   (0.4999 / 0.5001) from tearing down the graph every tick. Retuning `hz`
   properly means three biquad frequencies *plus* recomputing the make-up gain
   that `tone.ts:99-107` warns about getting wrong — so leave it quantized.

### And never bind band text

`bandText(m)` feeds `labels.get(spec.label)`, and `LabelFactory.cache` is
unbounded with a `CanvasTexture` per string. The same finite-codomain rule
applies, plus the same generation sweep, before any live number goes on a band.

