# Part 5 — The Panel under live data

Decision 3 is "live fields locked, rest editable", and it generalizes a
mechanism that already exists rather than adding one.

Already present: `Form.tsx:113-114` computes
`const locked = module.layout.locked === true` and
`const disabledFor = (path) => locked && path !== 'layout.locked'` — a
**per-path** predicate already threaded through every control. `layout.locked` is
in the schema; the warning banner is rendered at `Form.tsx:123-126`; the key
`com_ui_system_core_locked` exists at `translation.json:2077`; `List.tsx:101`
already draws a lock badge.

So: `BINDINGS` is keyed by the same dotted paths `Form.tsx` already enumerates,
and `Form` gains `boundAt(path)` alongside `disabledFor(path)`. When `live` is
null nothing is locked, so the feature-off path and every existing test keep
working with no special-casing.

**The subtle correctness point.** `Fields.tsx:1-27` documents that controls are
deliberately **uncontrolled**: the DOM holds the edit in progress, each control
registers a `read()`, and `Form.commit` asks every control for its value at
once. Therefore a locked field must:

- render `readOnly`, **not** `disabled` — `disabled` greys a control to
  unreadability, and the point of a live field is that you can read it; and
  `readOnly` fires no `change` event, so the delegated listener at
  `Form.tsx:107` is untouched;
- **not register a reader.** If it did, `Form.commit` would write the stale DOM
  value over the live one on the next commit of any *other* field. By not
  registering, `commit`'s existing `if (read)` guard skips the path and the
  `structuredClone` preserves what the module holds — so **`commit` needs no
  change at all**.

Existing actions:

| Action | Effect |
|---|---|
| `replace` | unchanged — bound paths register no reader, so a commit cannot carry a live value in |
| `add` | unchanged; a new module has no reading so everything is editable, and it *becomes* bound if its id ever matches a target — a useful manual-binding affordance worth documenting |
| `remove` | records a dismissal so the catalogue does not resurrect it |
| `reset` | re-seeds from the catalogue when configured, else the fixture; clears dismissals, runtime, lane sequence, selection |
| pin-phase | unchanged, and an argument for the layering: `motion.phase` is never bound |
| copy-JSON | unchanged and correct — it emits the **authored** arrangement; worth a comment saying so |

New chrome: one header chip showing poll state (`live` / `stale` / `paused` /
`off`) with the last-update age, and a retry button in the error state.
`List.tsx:97`'s swatch must use the *effective* status, or the legend contradicts
the bands it is a legend for — which `List.tsx:1-7` explicitly cares about.

Free reuse: `meta.group` set from the Prometheus job gives `List.tsx:31-45`
grouping and headings at no cost, including an "overflow" section; `layout.order`
from the catalogue gives deterministic ordering, which also removes lane-shuffle
risk, since `runtimeFor` assigns `lane = laneSeq % LANES` in list order.

Overflow lane: reserve the outermost ring (`OVERFLOW_LANE = LANES - 1 = 4`,
consistent with `schema.ts:220` bounding `motion.lane` to `max: 4`), carried as
`meta.group === 'overflow'` so no schema change is needed and a user can promote
a module out of it by hand. **None of this is visible until `Strip.tsx:253-258`
is uncommented.**

