# Part 6 — Access control

Decision 4 is `SystemCapabilities.ACCESS_ADMIN`, and the enforcement point is
the endpoint:

```js
router.use(requireJwtAuth, requireCapability(SystemCapabilities.ACCESS_ADMIN));
```

`packages/api/src/middleware/capabilities.ts` returns 401 with no user and **403
`{message:'Forbidden'}`** when the capability is absent, before the handler runs,
so an unauthorized caller never triggers an upstream query.

Admins already hold it: `seedSystemGrants()`
(`packages/data-schemas/src/methods/systemGrant.ts:516`) grants the ADMIN role
every capability at startup, idempotently. `AuthService.js:412` makes the first
registered user ADMIN, and `admin/users.ts:143` / `admin/roles.ts:541` prevent
demoting the last one.

**The client cannot evaluate this.** `SystemCapabilities` is backend-only and
never re-exported through `librechat-data-provider`; `TUser` has no capabilities
field; `useHasAccess` reads the unrelated `PermissionTypes` system. Duplicating
`user?.role === SystemRoles.ADMIN` on the client would also diverge from the
server the moment someone grants `ACCESS_ADMIN` to a non-admin role through the
existing grants UI — the API would serve them while the button stayed hidden.

So add **`GET .../availability`** returning `{ enabled, configured }` with
`enabled = configured && hasCapability(user, ACCESS_ADMIN)`, fetched once per
session (`staleTime: Infinity`). Precedent: `useGetSearchEnabledQuery`
(`Misc/queries.ts:41`) exists for exactly this "fire a query to decide whether to
show an affordance" purpose. Three consumers: `SystemCoreButton.tsx`
(early-return null), `useUnifiedSidebarLinks.ts:73-81` (omit the link), and the
poll's `enabled`.

The gate is on **discovery, not on the modal**: `SystemCoreDialog` stays mounted
and stays functional on the fixture, so the 3D toy remains harmless to anyone who
opens it by other means while the *data* is privileged.

